 "title": "CAP theorem",
 "sources": [
  "Wikipedia, 'CAP theorem' (secondary source, fetched 2026-11-17): any distributed data store can provide at most two of three guarantees: consistency (every read receives the most recent write or an error), availability (every request received by a non-failing node must result in a response), partition tolerance (the system continues to operate despite an arbitrary number of messages being dropped or delayed); if there is a network partition, one has to choose between consistency or availability; conjecture by Eric Brewer (PODC 2000), formal proof by Seth Gilbert and Nancy Lynch (2002); in 2012 Brewer added that designers only need to sacrifice consistency or availability in the presence of partitions. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CAP_theorem",
  "Engineering illustration, not from the source: payments refuse when unsure, a like counter may show a stale number."
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Your database runs on two servers, the network between them breaks, and now they can't talk. What should happen?",
   "caps": "*TWO SERVERS | NETWORK *BREAKS | NOW WHAT?"
  },
  {
   "text": "That's the CAP theorem: a distributed data store can give you at most two of three guarantees.",
   "caps": "THE *CAP THEOREM | AT MOST *TWO OF THREE"
  },
  {
   "text": "Consistency means every read gets the most recent write, or an error.",
   "caps": "*CONSISTENCY | LATEST WRITE *OR AN ERROR"
  },
  {
   "text": "Availability means every request to a working node gets a response.",
   "caps": "*AVAILABILITY | EVERY REQUEST *GETS AN ANSWER"
  },
  {
   "text": "Partition tolerance means the system keeps working even when messages between nodes get dropped or delayed.",
   "caps": "*PARTITION TOLERANCE | MESSAGES *DROPPED | KEEPS *WORKING"
  },
  {
   "text": "So when the network splits, you must choose: refuse to answer and stay consistent, or answer and risk stale data.",
   "caps": "NETWORK *SPLITS | *REFUSE, CONSISTENT | OR *ANSWER, STALE"
  },
  {
   "text": "But here's the catch: it's not a permanent choice of two. Brewer later said you only give one up during a partition.",
   "caps": "BUT THERE IS A *CATCH | NOT *PERMANENT | ONLY DURING A *PARTITION"
  },
  {
   "text": "So decide per feature: a payment should refuse when unsure, while a like count can show a stale number.",
   "caps": "DECIDE *PER FEATURE | PAYMENT: *REFUSE | LIKES: *STALE IS FINE"
  }
 ],
